cff-version: 1.2.0
message: Please cite the deposited preprint. Archive version 0.13.0 contains manuscript 0.15.0-draft29.
title: 'The cortical column as a tuned receiver: a network mechanism for temporal-interference
  stimulation'
abstract: 'We develop a mesoscale model of temporal-interference (TI) stimulation with three
  stages in series: membrane access, nonlinear detection of the difference frequency absent
  from the field, and circuit amplification. We represent access by an effective membrane time
  and analyze detection and amplification in a Jansen–Rit column, an exact mean field of quadratic
  integrate-and-fire (QIF) neurons, and its spiking network. We compare spatial maps in one
  head model and quantify conditional kHz access budgets. For weak fields, a static quadratic
  detector generates a beat proportional to the carrier-polarization product and a signed, direct-current-like
  (DC-like) steady source proportional to their summed squares. Transcranial alternating-current
  (tACS) and direct-current (tDCS) stimulation sources are linear to leading order. At the pinned
  Jansen–Rit operating point, 0.1–0.3 mV per carrier gives TI sources approximately 2–5% of
  direct sources at equal per-carrier and direct-input polarizations. Dynamic QIF detection
  crosses from static curvature to positive-slope rectification at fast carriers. Changing the
  current ratio moves the hippocampal envelope and mean-square centroids oppositely but only
  rescales the beat map at fixed state. Near a Hopf bifurcation, resonant gain grows inversely
  with modal damping and is shared with tACS. In the pinned Jansen–Rit column, coupling changes
  gain independently of detection; amplification appears mainly in beat-locked population-rate
  modulation, with little mean-rate change. At stronger inputs, the steady source alone drives
  the circuit across its gamma threshold, as does an unmodulated carrier matched in root-mean-square
  (RMS) input. Weak-field beat dose follows the polarization product rather than the envelope
  minimum. The steady source can act at depth; its sign depends on detector state and dynamics,
  not carrier polarity. For uniform scalar coupling and detection in homogeneous conductivity,
  its magnitude has no strict interior maximum. Polarization measurements and amplitude and
  frequency sweeps test the stages; physiological kHz coupling remains uncalibrated.'
